# Dependencies
node_modules/

# Environment variables / secrets — never commit real credentials
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# Real business configuration (not a template) — kept out of git normally so this
# codebase stays reusable for a different business without a code change (CLAUDE.md
# rule 14). Committed anyway for this repo (private, owner-confirmed) so hosting
# platforms that deploy straight from GitHub (e.g. Railway) have a real config to
# start from - no credentials live in this file, only business facts.
# configuration/business.yaml

# Persisted run history (agent/core/runHistoryStore.js) and other real per-business
# data - these READMEs already documented this as git-ignored (see
# memory/state/README.md, data/README.md) before there was any actual data or rule
# here to back that up; this closes that gap now that runHistoryStore.js is the first
# thing that actually writes real business/run data to these folders. Each folder's
# own README.md is kept (it's documentation, not data).
memory/state/runs/

# Persisted Command Center sessions (agent/core/commandCenterSessionStore.js) - one JSON
# file per session, holding a real conversation about this business's real catalogue and
# results. Same category of data as the run records above, ignored for the same reason.
# The store refuses to write a credential into one, but the business content itself is
# still never committed.
memory/state/sessions/

# Persisted, business-isolated Memory layer records (agent/core/memoryStore.js) - one
# JSON file per verified/approved fact under memory/state/business/<businessId>/records/.
# Real business memory, never a template - never commit.
memory/state/business/

# Durable pending approvals and the actions they gate (approvals/approvalStore.js) - one
# JSON file per approval under memory/state/approvals/. Each carries a real execution
# request (objective, tool, business_id) and, once decided, the verified approval
# provenance. The store refuses to write a credential into one, but the business content
# and the signed decision are still never committed. Added with that store, the same way
# the three rules above were added with whatever first wrote real data to their folder.
memory/state/approvals/

# Persisted monitoring snapshots (monitoring/snapshotStore.js) - one JSON file per capture
# under memory/state/snapshots/<businessKey>/<platform>/. Each is a real picture of a real
# store's catalogue, stock and order/customer counts at a moment in time. Same category of
# data as the records above, ignored for the same reason, and added with the store that
# first writes to that folder.
memory/state/snapshots/

# Persisted scheduled jobs (scheduler/scheduleStore.js) - one JSON file per job under
# memory/state/schedules/<businessKey>/. Each names a real business's recurring work and
# carries the claim state that stops an occurrence running twice. Same category of data as
# the records above, ignored for the same reason.
memory/state/schedules/

# Persisted reliability state (reliability/circuitBreaker.js, reliability/executionVerification.js) -
# which of a business's actions are currently failing, and which consequential changes have
# already been applied and verified. Real per-business operational data, and the verification
# records are what stop a consequential action being applied twice - never committed.
memory/state/circuits/
memory/state/verifications/

data/business/*
!data/business/README.md
data/products/*
!data/products/README.md
data/tasks/*
!data/tasks/README.md

# Logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*

# OS / editor junk
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.vscode/
.idea/

# Build/cache output (future-proofing; safe to keep even if unused today)
dist/
build/
coverage/
.claude/

.vercel
.env*
